---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import StoreButtons from '../../components/StoreButtons.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

// The iPhone launch announcement. Dated post, listed on /blog. Its job is a
// timely, shareable URL for the launch (Reddit, X, press) and a freshness
// signal, and to point iPhone readers at the App Store and the /iphone page.
const path = '/blog/i-like-movies-is-now-on-iphone';
const title = 'I Like Movies is now on iPhone';
const description =
  'I Like Movies is on the App Store. The same free movie and TV tracker, with your library synced across iPhone and Android and shared with your household.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Alexandru Craciun' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
    mainEntityOfPage: new URL(path, SITE.url).href,
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  title={`${title} | ${SITE.name}`}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  heading={title}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  disclosure={false}
>
  <p>
    I Like Movies is on the App Store. After a year on Android, the same app is
    now on iPhone, free, with no ads and nothing to upgrade. If you have been
    waiting for it, this is the day.
  </p>

  <div class="cta-row">
    <StoreButtons />
  </div>

  <h2>The same app, not a cut-down one</h2>
  <p>
    The iPhone app is the app, not a lite version of it. Films and TV in one
    library tracked down to the episode, private ratings and notes, monthly
    Discover collections built from your own ratings, an assistant you can ask
    in plain language, streaming availability for your country, and a household
    that shares one watchlist and one watched history. Everything the Android
    app does, the iPhone app does.
  </p>

  <h2>Your library is already there</h2>
  <p>
    Because your library lives on the server rather than on the handset, an
    account you started on Android is already waiting when you sign in on an
    iPhone, and the other way around. A household now shares one library across
    both platforms, so a mixed-phone home finally works the way it should: a
    film one person adds is on everyone's list before the evening starts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Coming from another app, a Letterboxd .zip or an IMDb .csv imports your
    ratings and watchlist directly, so a library built over years does not start
    from zero.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>
  <p>
    Download it from the <a href={SITE.appStoreUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">App Store</a>,
    or read the <a href="/iphone">iPhone page</a> for what it does and how it
    compares. If you are weighing it against what you use now, the{' '}
    <a href="/guides">guides and comparisons</a> lay that out honestly, including
    where the other app is the better choice.
  </p>
  <p>
    I build this because I use it, and it gets better fastest when someone tells
    me where it is wrong. If you have opinions on what a movie tracker should do
    on iPhone, I want them.
  </p>

  <div class="cta-row">
    <StoreButtons />
    <a href="/">More about the app</a>
  </div>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .cta-row {
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
  }
</style>
